Relay a unix socket to connected clients, and tell them the capture size.

--tool-state-socket connects to a socket and forwards each newline-delimited
message to the client verbatim, retrying while the socket does not exist and
reconnecting when the peer goes away. Weylus does not interpret the payload.

Clients also get the size of the capture, which the encoded video may be
smaller than, so they can map remote pixels to display pixels.

